<memory_item>
<description>
For "fill in the operators" problems, first use quick reasoning to narrow candidates (e.g., a negative target suggests subtraction or a negative intermediate), then confirm with exhaustive brute-force. Reasoning helps you sanity-check the code output and communicate your approach, but the brute-force is the reliable method to guarantee correctness.
</description>
<example>
Q1: Target is -205 (negative and large magnitude). Reasoning suggests multiplication might be involved to reach large magnitude, and subtraction to get negative. But checking 19×8=152, 19×8×28=4256 — no combination reaches -205. Brute-force confirms no solution exists.
</example>
</memory_item>
<memory_item>
<description>
When reasoning about operator-fill problems, consider the sign and magnitude of the target to prune the search space. A negative target requires an odd number of subtractions (in additive contexts) or a negative intermediate from subtraction. A large-magnitude target suggests multiplication. This reasoning can quickly eliminate impossible cases before running code.
</description>
<example>
For 19 ? 8 ? 28 = -205: The magnitude 205 is larger than any additive combination (max |19-8-28| = 17, max sum = 55), so pure addition/subtraction cannot reach it. Multiplication is needed for large magnitude, but 19×8×28 = 4256 is too large and 19×8±28 = 180/124 are positive. This confirms no solution exists.
</example>
</memory_item>
